{"schemaVersion":1,"sourceRecord":{"schemaVersion":1,"recordId":"wikibooks-200054-r4516624","sourceId":"wikibooks-cookbook-cc-by-sa","sourceTitle":"Cookbook:Spice Rub","sourcePageId":200054,"sourceRevision":"4516624","sourceRevisionTimestamp":"2025-06-20T19:11:00Z","sourceRevisionUser":"Kittycataclysm","sourceUrl":"https://en.wikibooks.org/w/index.php?title=Cookbook:Spice%20Rub&oldid=4516624","retrievedAt":"2026-10-13T16:17:37.967Z","sourceCategories":["Category:Recipes","Category:Recipes for spice mixes","Category:Recipes using cayenne","Category:Recipes without cooking time","Category:Recipes without servings","Category:Very Easy recipes"],"license":{"id":"wikibooks-cookbook-cc-by-sa-4.0","name":"Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International (CC BY-SA 4.0); Wikibooks text is generally dual-licensed with GFDL unless otherwise noted","evidenceUrl":"https://en.wikibooks.org/wiki/Wikibooks:Copyrights","policyStatus":"verified-license","pageExceptionStatus":"not-reviewed-per-page","attribution":"Attribute Wikibooks contributors by linking each version-specific source page. Indicate that structured fields are extracted and any translated or edited text is modified. Include CC BY-SA 4.0 notice/link and share adaptations under CC BY-SA 4.0 or later.","changeNoticeRequired":true,"shareAlikeRequired":true},"sourceSha256":"2c26a75d35a2f091be480c0b028def2ced93e403b01be1f4238348a6c341c159","sourceWikitext":"{{Recipe summary\n| Category = Spice mix recipes\n| Difficulty = 1\n}}\n\n{{recipe}}\n\nA '''spice rub''' is a mixture of salt, herbs, and spices. It can be as simple as a large amount of pepper, like in Steak au Poivre, or as complex as 100 spices. One formula for a good spice rub is a base, such as paprika, and then add on from there. However, beware commercial spice mixtures. Many of them are salt-based because salt fills volume, is cheap, and fills in for spices they can't get.\n\nIn order to make a successful rub, you need to balance flavors. You can often make rubs in bulk because spices last close to forever, and this avoids having to make another batch every two days—when you find a good rub, you will use it often. Also, if you're making a rub for one certain item, you'll want to pick flavors that work well with what you're making it for. Pork goes well with brown sugar, rosemary, and chipotles. Chicken goes with the same things as pork and things like lemon, lime, orange, cayenne pepper, and dry mustard powder. Beef works well with all of these. Paprika seems to work with just about everything.\n\nHere is a recipe for an all-purpose rub.\n\n==Ingredients==\n* 1 [[Cookbook:Cup|cup]] [[Cookbook:Paprika|paprika]]\n* ¼ cup [[Cookbook:Salt|salt]]\n* ¼ cup [[Cookbook:Pepper|black pepper]]\n* ½ cup [[Cookbook:Chipotle Pepper|chipotle]] powder\n* ½ cup [[Cookbook:Brown Sugar|brown sugar]]\n* ¾ cup dehydrated lemon peel\n* ¼ cup dried [[Cookbook:Rosemary|rosemary]]\n* 2 [[Cookbook:Tablespoon|tbsp]] [[Cookbook:Cayenne Pepper|cayenne pepper]]\n\n==Procedure==\n#Combine all ingredients in an airtight container.\n#Stored in a cool, dark place this will last for a long time.\n\n[[Category:Recipes for spice mixes]]\n[[Category:Recipes using cayenne]]","isRecipe":true,"recipe":{"name":"Spice Rub","nameAr":null,"servings":null,"servingsSourceText":null,"ingredients":[{"id":"source-line-1","name":"1 cup paprika","sourceText":"1 cup paprika","amountText":"1 cup paprika","amount":null,"unit":null,"foodBinding":null},{"id":"source-line-2","name":"¼ cup salt","sourceText":"¼ cup salt","amountText":"¼ cup salt","amount":null,"unit":null,"foodBinding":null},{"id":"source-line-3","name":"¼ cup black pepper","sourceText":"¼ cup black pepper","amountText":"¼ cup black pepper","amount":null,"unit":null,"foodBinding":null},{"id":"source-line-4","name":"½ cup chipotle powder","sourceText":"½ cup chipotle powder","amountText":"½ cup chipotle powder","amount":null,"unit":null,"foodBinding":null},{"id":"source-line-5","name":"½ cup brown sugar","